[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 703,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 466,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105152,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 466,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epibulbar choristoma is a benign congenital developmental lesion of histologically mature tissue in an abnormal location. Different subtypes can be distinguished: epibulbar dermoid is composed of dermal and epidermal elements, dermolipoma additionally contains a large amount of adipose tissue, and complex choristoma contains two or more ectopic tissues normally absent from the conjunctiva and caruncle.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:22:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 704,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 467,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105153,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 467,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:22:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 705,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 468,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105154,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 468,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LA14.06 Epibulbar choristoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:22:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 706,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 469,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105155,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 469,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> dermoid tumour; mixed dermoid.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:22:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 707,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 470,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105156,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epibulbar dermoid; dermolipoma; complex choristoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:22:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105157,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epibulbar choristomas are typically located in the superior or inferior temporal quadrants of the epibulbar compartment. If extensive, they can occupy the entire temporal epibulbar/conjunctival quadrant and may also involve parts of the cornea. They are occasionally found mainly on the cornea, with a minimal conjunctival component at the limbus {15350332}.</p>\r\n<p>Bilateral involvement is rare, but it can occur in syndromic patients {9709758; 27405313}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:22:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105158,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Dermoids are usually inferotemporal limbal, whitish, well-defined, firm lesions with cilia (eyelash hairs). They can sometimes be bilobed. A lipid line can be seen in the cornea in limbal dermoids.</p>\r\n<p>Dermolipomas are superotemporal yellowish, soft, non-mobile lesions that may contain cilia. They are usually less well defined than a dermoid and can be pedunculated or extend into the orbit.</p>\r\n<p>The features of complex choristomas depend on their underlying tissue composition.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:22:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105159,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epibulbar choristomas are rare. The prevalence in the population is unknown, but in one large series they constituted 2% of melanocytic and non-melanocytic conjunctival lesions {15350332}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:22:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105160,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epibulbar choristomas are developmental anomalies. They are frequently observed in patients with Goldenhar syndrome or linear naevus sebaceus syndrome {3200959; 25772967; 3684203}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:22:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105161,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:22:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105162,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em> (above).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:22:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105163,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Dermoids are composed of interweaving dermal-type collagen bundles covered by squamous epithelium (keratinized or non-keratinized, with or without goblet cells). Dermal appendages, in particular cilia with associated sebaceous glands and sweat glands, may be present. Variable amounts of fat can be observed.</p>\r\n<p>Dermolipomas additionally contain a large amount of adipose tissue. Skin appendages, in particular cilia with associated sebaceous glands and sweat glands, are found less frequently and are less numerous than in epibulbar dermoids.</p>\r\n<p>In complex choristomas, various additional heterotopic tissues can be found; lacrimal glands and cartilage are commonly encountered. Other components include smooth muscle {9709758; 2655139} and nerve fascicles {9709758}; more rarely there may be myxomatous tissue {21885965}, bone {3681614}, striated muscle {2655139}, immature tooth, and brain {166560}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:22:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105164,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:22:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
